import FormalSystem.Semantics.Nomic.Frame
import FormalSystem.Semantics.IntNormalForm

/-!
# `def:nomic-witnesses`: the ℤ-time presentation of a nomic frame

Over ℤ a nomic frame is determined by a *family* of one-step relations `R_w`, with
`⇒ʷ_n = (R_w)^n` for `n ≥ 0` and the reflection convention of `def:task-relation` for `n < 0` —
which is exactly `def:nomic-witnesses`, and exactly what `FrameOver.ofStep` already does one
relation at a time. `ofStepFamily` assembles the family, and the whole point of the construction
is that

    (ofStepFamily R fwd bwd).at w = FrameOver.ofStep (R w) (fwd w) (bwd w)

holds **by `rfl`** (`ofStepFamily_at`), so every ℤ-normal-form lemma applies to each member
unchanged, with no transport. In particular `Saturation` comes from
`TaskFrame.saturation_of_finite` through `FrameOver.ofStep_isRegular`, which is the route the
paper's finite witnesses take; it is choice-dependent, and `ofStepFamily_isRegular` inherits that
cost rather than hiding it.

## Two presentation facts worth recording at the top

`ofStepFamily_isRegular`'s field is discharged by the **bare term**
`FrameOver.ofStep_isRegular (R w) (fwd w) (bwd w)`, not by rewriting with `ofStepFamily_at`: the
rewrite route fails to synthesize `Finite (ofStepFamily R fwd bwd).World`, since that carrier is
not syntactically `W` until the rewrite has already happened.

`mem_lawHist_ofStepFamily_iff` goes through `refine Iff.trans … ?_` rather than `rw`. The
membership's binder has type `↑intOrder → (ofStepFamily …).World`, which `rw` will not match
against a `ℤ → W` binder, while `refine`/`exact` close the gap up to defeq — the same pattern
`Semantics/SlicedFrame.lean` records at its own carrier boundary.

## Main Definitions

- `NomicFrame.ofStepFamily` — `def:nomic-witnesses`: the nomic frame over ℤ generated by a family
  of one-step relations

## Main Results

- `NomicFrame.ofStepFamily_at` — the `w`-th member is `FrameOver.ofStep (R w)`, by `rfl`
- `NomicFrame.mem_lawHist_iff_isStepPath` — `H_F^w` over ℤ is the set of bi-infinite `⇒ʷ`-paths
- `NomicFrame.mem_lawHist_ofStepFamily_iff` — and in the generated case, the set of `R_w`-paths
- `NomicFrame.lawHist_mono_of_le` — pointwise containment of one-step relations is containment of
  law-histories
- `NomicFrame.stepPath_invariant` — a two-sided step-invariant predicate holding anywhere along a
  step-path holds everywhere along it
- `NomicFrame.stepPath_forward_invariant` — its forward-only companion, by `Int.leInduction`

## Choice dependence

`ofStepFamily_isRegular` is choice-dependent: each member's *Saturation* is
`TaskFrame.saturation_of_finite`, which costs `Classical.choice`, as `FrameOver.ofStep`'s own
docstring records. The two `stepPath_*` invariants and the two membership bridges are choice-free
— they are inductions and definitional unfoldings — so a witness proof pays for choice exactly
once, at the frame, and not again per lemma.
-/

namespace FormalSystem.Semantics

namespace NomicFrame

/--
`def:nomic-witnesses`: the nomic frame over ℤ generated by a family of one-step relations, one per
world state, with `⇒ʷ_n = (R_w)^n` and the reflection convention for `n < 0`.

The two hypotheses are the paper's own — "each `R_w` below is total and surjective" — and they are
the *only* genuine obligations a ℤ-time witness carries: *Compositionality* comes from `iter_add`,
*Limit* from ℤ being a `SuccOrder`, and *Saturation* from finiteness of the carrier. They are
named `_fwd`/`_bwd` because they are consumed only by the lemmas below, not in this body, which
follows `FrameOver.ofReflectiveRegular`'s `_hcomp` convention.
-/
def ofStepFamily {W : Type} [Finite W] [Nonempty W] (R : W → W → W → Prop)
    (_fwd : ∀ w p, ∃ q, R w p q) (_bwd : ∀ w q, ∃ p, R w p q) : NomicFrame intOrder where
  World := W
  PosRel := fun w p x q => ofStepRel (R w) p (x : ℤ) q

/--
The `w`-th member of a generated family is `FrameOver.ofStep (R w)` — **by `rfl`**, not up to a
transport. This is the fact that makes every ℤ-normal-form lemma (`ofStep_step`,
`mem_HF_iff_adjacent`, `isTotal_respects_iff_adjacent`, `taskRel_eq_iter`) apply to each member
unchanged.
-/
theorem ofStepFamily_at {W : Type} [Finite W] [Nonempty W] {R : W → W → W → Prop}
    {fwd : ∀ w p, ∃ q, R w p q} {bwd : ∀ w q, ∃ p, R w p q} (w : W) :
    (ofStepFamily R fwd bwd).at w = FrameOver.ofStep (R w) (fwd w) (bwd w) := rfl

/--
A generated family is regular: each member is `FrameOver.ofStep (R w)`, whose four constraints
`FrameOver.ofStep_isRegular` already discharges from bi-seriality alone.

Discharged by the bare term, never by `rw [ofStepFamily_at w]` — see this module's docstring for
the synthesis failure that route provokes.

**Choice dependence, recorded honestly.** Each member's *Saturation* is
`TaskFrame.saturation_of_finite`, which costs `Classical.choice`. This instance inherits that
cost; `rmk:choice` does not cover it.

Constraints consumed: Compositionality, Seriality, Limit, Saturation
-/
instance ofStepFamily_isRegular {W : Type} [Finite W] [Nonempty W] (R : W → W → W → Prop)
    (fwd : ∀ w p, ∃ q, R w p q) (bwd : ∀ w q, ∃ p, R w p q) :
    (ofStepFamily R fwd bwd).IsRegular where
  regular w := FrameOver.ofStep_isRegular (R w) (fwd w) (bwd w)

/--
`H_F^w` over ℤ is exactly the set of bi-infinite `⇒ʷ`-step-paths.

One term, no tactic: `lawHist` is *literally* the left-hand side of
`FrameOver.isTotal_respects_iff_adjacent` at `F.at w`. That sibling, rather than
`FrameOver.mem_HF_iff_adjacent`, is the primary bridge here precisely because `lawHist` is already
the all-pairs predicate on a bare function, so no `WorldHistory` detour is needed;
`mem_HF_iff_adjacent` stays reachable through `NomicFrame.mem_lawHist_iff`.

Constraints consumed: Compositionality, Seriality, Limit
-/
theorem mem_lawHist_iff_isStepPath (F : NomicFrame intOrder) [F.IsRegular] (w : F.World)
    (τ : ℤ → F.World) : τ ∈ F.lawHist w ↔ IsStepPath (F.at w) τ :=
  FrameOver.isTotal_respects_iff_adjacent (F.at w) τ

/--
In a generated family, `H_F^w` is exactly the set of bi-infinite `R_w`-paths: the characterization
every witness proof below works with.

Routed through `refine Iff.trans … ?_` rather than `rw`, for the carrier-boundary reason recorded
in this module's docstring.

Constraints consumed: Compositionality, Seriality, Limit, Saturation
-/
theorem mem_lawHist_ofStepFamily_iff {W : Type} [Finite W] [Nonempty W] {R : W → W → W → Prop}
    {fwd : ∀ w p, ∃ q, R w p q} {bwd : ∀ w q, ∃ p, R w p q} (w : W) (τ : ℤ → W) :
    τ ∈ (ofStepFamily R fwd bwd).lawHist w ↔ ∀ n : ℤ, R w (τ n) (τ (n + 1)) := by
  refine Iff.trans (mem_lawHist_iff_isStepPath (ofStepFamily R fwd bwd) w τ) ?_
  exact forall_congr' fun n => FrameOver.ofStep_step (R w) (fwd w) (bwd w) (τ n) (τ (n + 1))

/--
Monotonicity: if every `R_v`-step is an `R_u`-step then every law-history at `v` is a law-history
at `u`.

Two lines on top of `mem_lawHist_ofStepFamily_iff`, and the lemma that lets a witness argue
`H_F^a ⊆ H_F^e` from `R_a ⊆ R_e` without re-running an induction.

Constraints consumed: Compositionality, Seriality, Limit, Saturation
-/
theorem lawHist_mono_of_le {W : Type} [Finite W] [Nonempty W] {R : W → W → W → Prop}
    {fwd : ∀ w p, ∃ q, R w p q} {bwd : ∀ w q, ∃ p, R w p q} {v u : W}
    (h : ∀ p q, R v p q → R u p q) :
    (ofStepFamily R fwd bwd).lawHist v ⊆ (ofStepFamily R fwd bwd).lawHist u := by
  intro τ hτ
  refine (mem_lawHist_ofStepFamily_iff u τ).mpr fun n => ?_
  exact h _ _ ((mem_lawHist_ofStepFamily_iff v τ).mp hτ n)

/--
**The two-sided step-path invariant.** A predicate preserved *in both directions* along every step
of a bi-infinite path, and holding at one time, holds at every time.

`Int.induction_on` in both directions from `n₀`, with `omega` — not `ring` — for the three index
identities: `ring` does not close `n₀ + (↑i + 1) = n₀ + ↑i + 1` at the shape it arrives in here.
The case names on this toolchain are `zero`/`succ`/`pred`, not the deprecated `hz`/`hp`/`hn`.

Choice-free: an induction over ℤ and nothing else.
-/
theorem stepPath_invariant {W : Type} {R : W → W → Prop} {τ : ℤ → W}
    (h : ∀ n : ℤ, R (τ n) (τ (n + 1))) {P : W → Prop}
    (hstep : ∀ p q, R p q → (P p ↔ P q)) (n₀ : ℤ) (h₀ : P (τ n₀)) (n : ℤ) : P (τ n) := by
  have key : ∀ k : ℤ, P (τ (n₀ + k)) := by
    intro k
    induction k using Int.induction_on with
    | zero => simpa using h₀
    | succ i ih =>
      have hi := hstep _ _ (h (n₀ + i))
      rw [show n₀ + ((i : ℤ) + 1) = n₀ + (i : ℤ) + 1 from by omega]
      exact hi.mp ih
    | pred i ih =>
      have hi := hstep _ _ (h (n₀ + (-(i : ℤ) - 1)))
      rw [show n₀ + (-(i : ℤ) - 1) + 1 = n₀ + -(i : ℤ) from by omega] at hi
      exact hi.mpr ih
  have hk := key (n - n₀)
  rwa [show n₀ + (n - n₀) = n from by omega] at hk

/--
**The forward-only companion.** A predicate merely *propagated forward* along each step — not
preserved backwards — and holding at `n₀`, holds at every later time.

The weaker hypothesis is what a separating argument of the form "once at `a`, always at `a`
forward" needs, and it is strictly weaker than `stepPath_invariant`'s: a relation can carry `P`
forward without carrying `¬P` forward, in which case no two-sided invariant is available. By
`Int.leInduction`, so the conclusion is bounded below by `n₀` rather than universal.

Choice-free: an induction over ℤ and nothing else.
-/
theorem stepPath_forward_invariant {W : Type} {R : W → W → Prop} {τ : ℤ → W}
    (h : ∀ n : ℤ, R (τ n) (τ (n + 1))) {P : W → Prop}
    (hstep : ∀ p q, R p q → P p → P q) {n₀ : ℤ} (h₀ : P (τ n₀)) :
    ∀ n, n₀ ≤ n → P (τ n) := by
  intro n hn
  induction n, hn using Int.leInduction with
  | base => exact h₀
  | succ k _ ih => exact hstep _ _ (h k) ih

end NomicFrame

end FormalSystem.Semantics
